WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.330
Your club’s ideas board says

2
00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.750
one thing: “Build an AI!”

3
00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.750
I love the energy.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.530
But good designers don’t start with AI.

5
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.680
They start with a person

6
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.780
who has a problem.

7
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.530
Today: design an AI solution, from a

8
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.710
real problem to a safe plan.

9
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.180
Start with a real problem near you, and

10
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.500
talk to the people who have it.

11
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.310
In a school like yours, the office

12
00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.910
answers the same questions all day:

13
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.790
when are exams, when’s the bus?

14
00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.630
New students and parents miss the

15
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:38.830
answers in the group chat.

16
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.130
Now the hard question: do

17
00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.230
you need AI at all?

18
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.880
A notice board or one pinned

19
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.860
message might solve this for free.

20
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.580
AI earns its place only

21
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.880
when that isn’t enough.

22
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.220
Here, questions come late at night,

23
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.400
in many words and languages.

24
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.470
Next, plan the data: where it comes

25
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.170
from, with whose permission.

26
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.250
Here, it’s the school’s timetable and

27
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.790
rules, shared by the office.

28
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.700
Nothing personal: no names,

29
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.280
marks or phone numbers.

30
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.940
And decide what the AI does: search those

31
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.420
documents, and answer only from them.

32
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.910
So, a design plan answers five questions.

33
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.810
Whose problem is it?

34
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.550
Is AI needed?

35
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.520
What data, whose permission, and

36
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.060
what will AI do with it?

37
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.430
And what happens when it’s wrong?

38
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.990
Like building a footbridge over a stream.

39
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.440
First, ask who crosses, and when.

40
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.810
Maybe stepping stones would do.

41
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.420
Then choose strong materials.

42
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.570
And plan for floods: a handrail, a

43
00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.990
sign, someone checking after storms.

44
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:53.700
Quick check!

45
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.420
When you design an AI solution,

46
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.520
what should come first?

47
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.960
The problem and its people.

48
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.340
Start with the tool, and

49
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.500
you’ll hunt for a problem.

50
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.300
Start with people, and you’ll build

51
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.840
something they actually use.

52
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:11.770
Then plan for harm.

53
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.850
Who could be hurt by a mistake?

54
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.860
A wrong bus time could strand

55
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.260
a student after dark.

56
00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.600
So the helper shows its source, says “I

57
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.720
don’t know, ask the office” when nothing

58
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.220
matches, and the office checks it weekly.

59
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.880
A person stays in charge.

60
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.650
In the app, take apart a finished

61
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.010
design: a helper that answers

62
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.210
questions about a school.

63
00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.530
Watch it search the documents, quote the

64
00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.250
one it used, and say when it doesn’t know.

65
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.750
Then edit a document, and

66
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.030
see the answer change.

67
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:48.910
Your turn.

68
00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.460
A student asks something that

69
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.940
isn’t in the documents.

70
00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.630
What should the helper do?

71
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.770
Say so, and point to a person: an honest

72
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.230
“I don’t know” beats a confident guess.

73
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.540
Remember: start with the problem and its

74
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.420
people, and ask if AI is needed.

75
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.280
Plan the data, with permission.

76
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.300
And plan for harm, so a

77
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.600
person stays in charge.

78
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.800
Try it yourself: write a

79
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.900
one-page plan for an AI that

80
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.440
helps your school or community.

81
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.980
Name the problem and its people,

82
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.120
and say if AI is needed.

83
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.810
Then plan the data, and plan for mistakes.
